 160	\begin{remark}[The printed attainment form of the symmetrization lemma]\label{rem:symmetric-strat-limit}
 161		The source prints Lemma~\ref{lem:symmetric-strat} in the following stronger form (\path{references/qpbt-paper/06_nonlocal_games_and_mipstar.tex}, lines~94--99): if $\mathfrak G$ is a symmetric game with $\operatorname{val}^*(\mathfrak G) = 1-\eps$ for some $\eps \ge 0$, then there exists a symmetric and projective strategy $\mathcal S = (\ket{\psi}, M)$ with $\operatorname{val}^*(\mathfrak G, \mathcal S) \ge 1-\eps$. This development does not assert that sentence anywhere. It is recorded verbatim, and unasserted, as the Lean proposition \texttt{PrintedSymmetricProjectiveAttainmentClaim}.
 162	
 163		Two independent defects separate it from the corrected statement. First, the argument of Lemma~\ref{lem:symmetric-strat} yields, for each $\eps' > \eps$, a symmetric projective strategy of value at least $1-\eps'$, and the conclusion at $\eps' = \eps$ does not follow: the dilated dimension $d$ may grow as $\eps' \downarrow \eps$, and the supremum defining $\operatorname{val}^*(\mathfrak G)$ in Definition~\ref{def:tensor-product-value} need not be attained by any strategy. Second, on the formal domain the printed sentence is false: Definition~\ref{def:symmetric-game} permits an empty answer alphabet, for which no strategy exists at all while $\operatorname{val}^*(\mathfrak G)$ is still defined as the totalized supremum of the empty set, so the hypotheses hold at $\eps = 1$ and the conclusion has no witness. The Lean declaration \texttt{not\_forall\_printed\-Symmetric\-Projective\-Attainment\-Claim} proves exactly this refutation of the universally quantified printed claim.
 164	
 165		Nothing downstream is lost. The symmetrization construction is value-preserving, so the printed conclusion does hold whenever the hypothesis supplies an actual strategy of value at least $1-\eps$ rather than only the value of the game; that is Lemma~\ref{lem:symmetric-strat-given-strategy}. Every use of the lemma in the source proceeds from such a strategy, or from a strict lower bound on the value, and therefore consumes only what the corrected statement provides. The full analysis, including the kernel-checked empty-answer counterexample, is in \cite{gap:qpbt_symmetrization-attainment}.
 166	\end{remark}
