1648	\begin{definition}[Canonical parameters of the Pauli basis test]\label{def:introparams}
1649		\lean{MIPStarRE.QPBT.introParamsC, MIPStarRE.QPBT.introParamsTuple, MIPStarRE.QPBT.AdmissibleParams.ofTuple, MIPStarRE.QPBT.introParams}
1650		\leanok
1651		\uses{thm:pauli}
1652		Let $a, b$ be the universal constants of Theorem~\ref{thm:pauli}, and let $c$ be the smallest \emph{even} integer that is at least $(b+a)/b$. For every integer $R \ge 4$, define the tuple $\mathsf{introparams}(R) = (q, m, d)$ by
1653		\begin{itemize}
1654			\item $q = 2^k$ for $k = c \lceil \log \log R \rceil + 1$,
1655			\item $m = 2^j$, where $j$ is the unique integer with $2^j \le c \lceil \log R \rceil + 1 < 2^{j+1}$,
1656			\item $d = 1$.
1657		\end{itemize}
1658		The letter $k$ here denotes the exponent of the field size and is unrelated to the simultaneity parameter $k$ of the classical game. With this choice the Pauli basis test certifies the presence of an $M = 2^m$-qudit maximally entangled state of local dimension $q$, equivalently an $(M \log q)$-qubit EPR state, and the choice $d = 1$ runs the low individual degree test multilinearly.
